The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (if_id >= ethsw->sw_attr.num_ifs) {
    dev_err(dev, "Invalid if_id %d in IRQ status\n", if_id);
    goto out;
}
```
Reason:
This early `goto out` skips the subsequent call to `dpsw_clear_irq_status()`, leaving the interrupt status set. As a result, the IRQ remains asserted and fires repeatedly, causing an interrupt storm.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (if_id >= ethsw->sw_attr.num_ifs) {
    dev_err(dev, "Invalid if_id %d in IRQ status\n", if_id);
    goto out_clear;
}
...
out_clear:
    err = dpsw_clear_irq_status(ethsw->mc_io, 0, ethsw->dpsw_handle,
                                DPSW_IRQ_INDEX_IF, status);
    if (err)
        dev_err(dev, "Can't clear irq status (err %d)\n", err);
out:
    return IRQ_HANDLED;
```
Reason:
By jumping to the new label `out_clear` instead of `out`, the interrupt status is always cleared, even when an invalid `if_id` is detected. This prevents the IRQ from being raised again and eliminates the interrupt storm.

